# Diagnosis report — ill-posed inverse problem K x = y

## Structured fields
noise_estimate: 0.0289475
noise_norm_estimate: 0.431307
effective_rank: 25
dpc_judgment: does_not_hold
noise_color: colored_ARp4
ar_coefficients: [ 1.0649 -0.2534  0.1113 -0.013 ]

**Noise estimate, unambiguously stated.** `noise_estimate` = 0.0289475 is the
estimated **standard deviation of the noise per data component** (point-wise
sigma). `noise_norm_estimate` = 0.431307 = sigma0 * sqrt(n) is the
expected Euclidean norm of the full noise vector (n = 222). Because the noise
is colored (see below), the per-component sigma refers to the point-wise
standard deviation of the noise process, which is also the flat level of the
spectral coefficients |u_i^T y| in the signal-free band (mean of b_i^2 over
i >= 26, an incoherent SVD basis). Both numbers are reported.

## 1. Input inspection
- K: 222 x 222 (square, m = n), float64, entries in [-0.0463, 0.0521],
  sigma_max(K) = 1.0000 (normalized), not symmetric. The singular vectors are
  spatially incoherent (about n/2 = 111 sign changes for every index i, i.e.
  they are not ordered by spatial frequency) — consistent with a synthetic
  operator built from random orthogonal factors with a prescribed spectrum.
- y: 222 components, range [-0.3085, 0.3468], std 0.1324. Dimensions compatible.

## 2. Spectral structure and Picard condition
- Singular values decay geometrically without a gap: s_i = 0.8575^i
  (s[0]=1.0, s[10]=0.215, s[60]=9.8e-5, s[100]=2.1e-7); numerical rank 195
  (s falls to LAPACK dust ~5e-14 beyond i = 194).
- The Fourier coefficients |u_i^T y| (column `fourier_coeff` of
  picard_data.csv, absolute values) decay until i ~ 26 and then plateau
  at the noise level while s_i keeps decaying; the smoothed Picard ratio
  |u_i^T y|/s_i grows from ~0.8 (i=0) to ~11 (i=40), ~113 (i=60) and >1e4
  (i>=80). The discrete Picard condition therefore **does not hold**
  (dpc_judgment: does_not_hold) and regularization is indispensable.

## 3. Noise level and noise color
- Point noise variance: mean of b_i^2 over the signal-free band
  (i >= 26) gives sigma0 = 0.02895 per component; expected noise
  norm ||eta|| = 0.4313.
- **The noise is colored, not white.** Evidence:
  (a) the residual of any reasonable fit is spatially smooth: its lag-1
      autocorrelation is 0.85 (raw GCV fit), essentially unchanged
      (0.80-0.85) for regularization parameters spanning two orders of
      magnitude — a white-noise residual would give ~0;
  (b) a Monte-Carlo reference (400 white-noise realizations passed through the
      same estimation filter) puts the white-noise lag-1 autocorrelation of
      this residual in [-0.13, 0.12]; the observed value is ~12 standard
      deviations outside — white noise is decisively rejected;
  (c) K has incoherent singular vectors, so a projection onto a large
      sub-space (~89% of the energy) preserves the spatial correlation of the
      noise; the observed smoothness therefore reflects the noise itself,
      not an artifact of the projection;
  (d) the omitted signal error beyond the GCV filter is negligible
      (norm ~0.02 vs residual norm ~0.4), so the residual is essentially pure
      noise and its autocorrelation is a reliable noise diagnostic.
- **Noise model:** an AR(p) model was fitted to the residual autocorrelation
  (Yule-Walker; order p <= 4 selected by AIC), identified iteratively
  (fit -> residual -> re-fit). Converged model: AR(4), phi = [ 1.0649 -0.2534  0.1113 -0.013 ].
  Whiteness check of the final whitened residual: max |acf_k| (k=1..6) =
  0.061 <= white band 0.135
  (lags: [ 0.048 -0.035  0.014  0.061  0.024  0.056]) —
  consistent with white.
- Consequence: GCV / ordinary least squares are derived under a white-noise
  assumption; applying them directly to the colored data is statistically
  suboptimal (the estimator loss is large: ||U^T C^-1 U - I/sigma0^2||_F is
  several times ||I/sigma0^2||_F). The problem is therefore solved in
  **whitened form** (K~ = A K, y~ = A y, A the AR whitening filter), where the
  noise is white by construction of the model.

## 4. Effective rank — operational definition
effective_rank = smallest k such that the whitened residual spectral energy
beyond k is statistically consistent with white noise of variance sig2w:
    ||b~[k:]||^2 <= chi2_0.95(nw - k) * sig2w,
computed on the whitened system (nw = 218). This yields
effective_rank = 25, in good agreement with the GCV filter
transition index on the raw problem (26) and with GCV-TSVD
(k = 30 whitened). Alternative definitions on the raw problem give values
up to ~150 only because they inherit the noise-variance estimation error; the
difference is within the sampling error of the variance estimate and does not
indicate hidden signal above the noise floor.

## 5. Consequences for the solution strategy
- Geometric spectral decay without a gap + violated Picard condition =>
  filter-type regularization is required (Tikhonov / TSVD / iterative).
- The colored noise must be whitened before parameter selection; the
  discrepancy target on the whitened scale is ||r~|| = sigw * sqrt(nw)
  with sigw = 0.01177 (estimated from the GCV fit residuals,
  sig2w = ||r~||^2 / (nw - df)).
